<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kievan Rus'</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kievan Rus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kievan Rus' was a medieval East Slavic state centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, flourishing from the late ninth to the thirteenth century. Its rulers attacked and helped destroy the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a> in the tenth century, removing a major power on the steppe. Fragmented into rival principalities, it was overrun by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> in the 1230s and 1240s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kievan-rus.jpg" alt="Kievan Rus'" width="320" height="269" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vitaliyf261 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_of_Kyivan_Rus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…turned potential enemies into clients, neutralised threats and projected Byzantine culture far beyond the reach of its armies — as shown by the mission of Saints Cyril and Methodius to the Slavs of Moravia in 863 and the conversion of Bulgaria (864) and of <b>Kievan Rus'</b> (c. 988).</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>These texts and the Cyrillic script passed to Serbia and to <b>Kievan Rus'</b> after its conversion in 988, and Old Church Slavonic became the liturgical and literary language of much of the Orthodox Slavic world; Cyrillic is today used for Bulgarian, Serbian, Russian, Ukrainian and many other languages.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>By drawing most of the western lands of <b>Kievan Rus'</b> away from Mongol and later Muscovite control, it created a lasting divide between these lands and Russia, with consequences for later religious and national identities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…carried it to Bulgaria, Croatia and Bohemia; at the Preslav Literary School Bulgarian disciples such as Naum and Clement of Ohrid created the Cyrillic alphabet, which became the script of the Bulgarian Empire and <b>Kievan Rus'</b>, and Old Church Slavonic remains the liturgical language of several Orthodox churches.</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Regionally, Khazar dominance over steppe and river networks shaped the development of emerging states such as <b>Kievan Rus</b>, whose early rulers even adopted the title khagan, and Khazar influence persisted in the political structures and trade patterns of successor polities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>As the second city of <b>Kievan Rus’</b>, Novgorod was usually governed by a son of the grand prince of Kiev: Vladimir the Great and Yaroslav the Wise both ruled there before taking the throne in Kiev, and the stone Cathedral of Saint Sophia was built in 1045–1050.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
